## 151: six graphical solution tasks, independently corroborated by algebra

Draw both stated loci and read ALL their intersections; approximate coordinates
are appropriate for a graph, while the exact coordinates below identify them.

- a) Draw line y=x-1 and hyperbola y=-12/x. Substitution gives
  x^2-x+12=0 with discriminant -47: no real intersections/solutions.
- b) Draw line y=8-x and circle centered at the origin of radius 9.
  2x^2-16x-17=0. Intersections are
  (4+7sqrt(2)/2,4-7sqrt(2)/2) and its coordinate reversal, approximately
  (8.950,-0.950),(-0.950,8.950).
- c) Draw line y=11-2x and circle of radius 5.
  5x^2-44x+96=0=(x-4)(5x-24). Intersections (4,3),(24/5,7/5),
  i.e. (4,3),(4.8,1.4).
- d) Draw parabola y=x^2 and line y=(3-3x)/2.
  2x^2+3x-3=0, so x=(-3+/-sqrt(33))/4 and y=x^2.
  Exactly ((-3+sqrt(33))/4,(21-3sqrt(33))/8) and
  ((-3-sqrt(33))/4,(21+3sqrt(33))/8), approximately
  (0.686,0.471),(-2.186,4.779).
- e) Draw parabola y=-x-x^2 and line y=x-10.
  x^2+2x-10=0. Intersections (-1+sqrt(11),-11+sqrt(11)) and
  (-1-sqrt(11),-11-sqrt(11)), approximately
  (2.317,-7.683),(-4.317,-14.317).
- f) Draw circle of radius sqrt(10) and hyperbola y=3/x.
  (x+y)^2=16 and (x-y)^2=4, with independently chosen signs.
  All four intersections: (3,1),(1,3),(-3,-1),(-1,-3).

All six are ungraded because the specified method includes graphical work.
An exact-list-only auto key would not assess the requested drawing/method.

