## 153: six polynomial systems, all real branches

- a) Let t=x+y. Then t^2=3 and x+3y=-1 imply
  y=-(1+t)/2 and x=(1+3t)/2. For t=+/-sqrt(3), exactly
  ((1+3sqrt(3))/2,(-1-sqrt(3))/2) and
  ((1-3sqrt(3))/2,(-1+sqrt(3))/2).
- b) y=2x-1 makes the second equation -2x^2+6x-1=-1;
  x(x-3)=0. Solutions (0,-1),(3,5); x=0 is allowed here.
- c) 2x=11-y makes the second equation 5+4y-y^2=0;
  (y-5)(y+1)=0. Solutions (3,5),(6,-1).
- d) x=y+4 makes -y^2+9y+12=26;
  (y-2)(y-7)=0. Solutions (6,2),(11,7).
- e) Use 4x^2-9y^2=(2x-3y)(2x+3y)=5(2x+3y).
  The first equation becomes 11x-25y-19=0. With 2x-3y=5,
  this gives y=1,x=4, exactly one solution. No quadratic second
  branch survives the fixed linear factor.
- f) x=y-2 gives 4y^2+9y-4=5;
  (4y-3)(y+3)=0. Solutions (-5/4,3/4),(-5,-3).

All reductions are reversible and no domains were narrowed. Finite coordinates
are eligible for auto numeric/expression grading with explicit ordering labels.

## 154: complete real-parameter no-solution condition

Under x+y=6, x^2+y^2=18+2(x-3)^2>=18.
Thus no real solution exactly when r^2<18, i.e.
-3sqrt(2)<r<3sqrt(2). The text says "what numbers r" and does not
restrict r to nonnegative radii. Omitting negative r would be an error.
At either endpoint r=+/-3sqrt(2), only (3,3) exists. For r^2>18,
the two points are (3+/-sqrt((r^2-18)/2),3-/+sqrt((r^2-18)/2)).
Condition/interval prose can be ungraded; automatic expression grading of
inequalities is not assumed.

## 155: complete real-parameter existence condition

Set s=x+y=c and d=x-y. Then d^2=200-c^2.
Real solutions exist exactly when c^2<=200, i.e.
-10sqrt(2)<=c<=10sqrt(2). At each endpoint there is one solution
x=y=c/2; in the interior there are two
((c+sqrt(200-c^2))/2,(c-sqrt(200-c^2))/2) and their reversals.
Outside the closed interval there is no real solution. All signs and closed
endpoints matter. Ungraded interval explanation is acceptable.

## 156: four rational systems; x!=0 and y!=0 throughout

- a) y=2x-5. The rational equation is equivalent, on its domain,
  to 6(x+y)=xy. Therefore 2x^2-23x+30=(2x-3)(x-10)=0.
  All solutions (10,15),(3/2,-2) have both coordinates nonzero.
- b) y=(14-x)/2. From 20(y-x)=xy obtain
  x^2-74x+280=(x-4)(x-70)=0.
  All solutions (4,5),(70,-28), both in the original domain.
- c) Mixed number 2 1/12 means 25/12, not 2/12 and not a product.
  Let p=xy. Then (196-2p)/p=25/12, hence p=48.
  With x+y=14, roots of t^2-14t+48=(t-6)(t-8) are 6,8.
  Both ordered pairs (6,8),(8,6) are valid and nonzero.
- d) x=y+2. Multiplication gives 6(x^2-y^2)=5xy, or
  12(x+y)=5xy. Thus 5y^2-14y-24=(y-4)(5y+6)=0.
  Both (6,4),(4/5,-6/5) satisfy the original rational equation.

No excluded zero root remains in any system. The negative branches in a,b,d
must not be discarded without a positivity premise. Separate finite
coordinate parts can be auto numeric/expression.

## 157: four factored systems, exhaustive branch splits

- a) x+y=0 gives (1/3,-1/3); x-y=0 gives (1,1).
- b) x=3 gives (3,4),(3,-4); y=5 gives (0,5). Exactly three.
- c) x=7y OR x=-7y. Each gives y^2=2. All four independent
  sign combinations (+/-7sqrt(2),+/-sqrt(2)) occur; no restriction
  forces the signs to agree.
- d) x=0 would give -y^2=50, impossible over the reals.
  The other branch y=-1 gives x^2=51, so
  (sqrt(51),-1),(-sqrt(51),-1), exactly two.

Finite solution-coordinate auto grading is appropriate with complete branches.

## 158: four symmetric systems

For S=x^2+y^2 and P=xy, set s=x+y and d=x-y:
s^2=S+2P, d^2=S-2P, and (x,y)=((s+d)/2,(s-d)/2).
All real sign choices of s,d must be included.

- a) S=40,P=-12: s^2=16,d^2=64. Exactly
  (6,-2),(-2,6),(-6,2),(2,-6).
- b) S=80,P=24: s^2=128,d^2=32. Exactly
  (6sqrt(2),2sqrt(2)),(2sqrt(2),6sqrt(2)),
  (-6sqrt(2),-2sqrt(2)),(-2sqrt(2),-6sqrt(2)).
- c) S=15,P=6: s^2=27,d^2=3. Exactly
  (2sqrt(3),sqrt(3)),(sqrt(3),2sqrt(3)),
  (-2sqrt(3),-sqrt(3)),(-sqrt(3),-2sqrt(3)).
- d) xy=-24 and x^2+y^2+xy=9 imply S=33.
  Then (x+y)^2=33-48=-15, impossible: the empty real solution set.

Finite coordinates and a clearly labeled empty-set key can be auto; ordering
labels must not imply only one pair or collapse separate x,y inputs.

